<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Habbari dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">854 CE – 1026 CE (approx.) · capital Al-Mansura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab dynasty that ruled lower Sindh from al-Mansura as nominal vassals of the Abbasids until Mahmud of Ghazni ended it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> had been an Umayyad and then Abbasid province since the conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> in 711 to 712. As Abbasid control weakened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-abd-al-aziz-al-habbari">Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Habbari</a>, from an Arab family settled in Sindh, made himself ruler around 854 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansura-sindh">al-Mansura</a>, the provincial capital near modern Shahdadpur, while continuing to recognise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Habbari <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-multan">Emirate of Multan</a> held the north. Arab geographers such as al-Masudi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansura-sindh">al-Mansura</a> as a prosperous city with a large mosque, where Arabic and Sindhi were spoken and trade passed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The ruling elite was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim, while much of the population remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1026 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansura-sindh">al-Mansura</a>, reportedly because its ruler had adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> beliefs, and deposed the Habbari emir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavids</a>, and soon after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/soomra-dynasty">Soomra dynasty</a> rose to power.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/habbari-dynasty.jpg" alt="Habbari dynasty" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan.Contouring and city names: Cattette · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Habbarid_Emirate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>854 CE – 1026 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Al-Mansura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Habbari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Sindhi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Mansura, Habbarids, Emirate of Sindh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=940&amp;focus=habbari-dynasty" aria-label="Habbari dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6466 1106 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Habbari dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6748 1217h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6786 1362h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6786 1362h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Pakistan. <a href="/atlas?year=940&amp;focus=habbari-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
